","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":31,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"similar passage Λ chapter 10 …. Elsewhere, however, Aristotle mostly rejects the idea of good in general as vacuous","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2047,2208,2089],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0032","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":32,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"and unhelpful ….\": but Aristotle always rejects the idea of the good (as of course did Speusippus), always accepts","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2095,2221,2137],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0033","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":33,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"the good-itself (with Plato and against Speusippus), never identifies them, and never mentions the idea of the good","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2143,2162,2185],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0034","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":34,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"in this passage (or anywhere else in the Metaphysics, except en passant as an example in Z6; on all this see Iα4","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2191,2220,2233],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0035","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":35,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"above). Annas thus treats an important common theme of N and Λ, and the most important case where Λ builds on","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2239,2155,2281],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0036","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":36,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"an aporia in N and adds a resolution, as a regrettable confusion which Aristotle later outgrew. we should credit","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2287,2253,2328],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0037","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":37,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"Jaeger’s perceptiveness in seeing the theme and its importance for Aristotle’s perception of his relations to Plato and","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2335,2220,2376],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0038","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":38,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"Speusippus: Jaeger was wrong to confine this to an early period, but perhaps the weight of standard interpretations","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2383,2206,2424],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0039","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":39,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"of Aristotle was such that Jaeger could perceive important aspects of the real Aristotle only by seeing them as not-","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2431,2200,2472],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0040","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":40,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"yet-fully-Aristotelian. Annas, however, manages to blind herself to what Jaeger had seen, by detaching the N text","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2478,2233,2520],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0041","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":41,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"from the Λ parallel, suggesting that the regrettable early confusion is confined to Λ and that Aristotle has overcome","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2527,2159,2568],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0042","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":42,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"it by the time of N. Annas also manages not to discuss the N passage in talking about Aristotle’s critique of the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2574,2242,2616],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0043","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":43,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"Academics in her introduction, presumably because her introduction is about philosophy of mathematics, which she","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2622,2108,2664],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0044","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":44,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"takes to be the only really important part of MN. {perhaps Annas thinks that Plato and Speusippus share the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2670,2211,2712],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0045","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":45,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"assumption, which Aristotle would reject, that there is a good-itself, and that they differ only about whether this is","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2718,2253,2760],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-native-0046","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf414-note-unresolved-1","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":46,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"an ἀρχή or generated afterwards. I think it would be quite surprising if Speusippus thought there was a good-itself at","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverifi